#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078250 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "e7ff1089-5799-5654-8e78-5c801a676aa1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6167" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta \\cot \\theta}{4 \\cos^2 \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{1}{4 \\cos \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\cos 0} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078299 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "7a06800b-021c-5e24-b25b-e0026730165a" :question/math-academy-id "e-94" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} .$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x \\to 0,$ both the numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d3c4e5f-fbae-5a33-9bdb-2e0715f1f501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383281} {:answer/id #uuid "69574a77-66da-5d9a-8b71-a654a9d4e77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383282} {:answer/id #uuid "aed7529e-d700-58d5-a5f1-a1c93190507a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383283} {:answer/id #uuid "96927685-6a85-5698-97cf-0f275157c475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383284} {:answer/id #uuid "db598533-959e-5159-871c-46bd87099160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d3c4e5f-fbae-5a33-9bdb-2e0715f1f501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383281} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0616614-92da-5bae-8f7a-67d2e95ccc9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37c2ffbf-c85d-57bc-b220-7911fe6a2307" :question/math-academy-id "q-105409" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244629a0-6b61-5a65-ae36-fab6860a5bd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383288} {:answer/id #uuid "8e30c4e8-9983-5693-bc89-04abe639d17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383289} {:answer/id #uuid "cd41cdea-3221-599c-816f-1730ff0d4382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383290} {:answer/id #uuid "5df462f7-abb7-5dc4-8d72-4c2716c99efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383291} {:answer/id #uuid "acc2c55a-e18d-52ac-bdf0-b333b7fddc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e30c4e8-9983-5693-bc89-04abe639d17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383289} :answer-field/id #uuid "5821d289-1a70-5ffa-8441-7239ffaf2a26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b7f6b18-e042-5b37-92b3-e2e8dfeb7c5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105426" :question/problem "${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }÷\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }÷\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68966577-c1d3-5fa0-bd79-53a39e4dfe9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68966577-c1d3-5fa0-bd79-53a39e4dfe9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383295} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a3d43fd-1060-51c4-8c36-841ed8cb0237" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c2bb74d-654b-5f71-a360-a61566e9b5c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-302827" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba51663a-cf49-5ab7-996a-75a703853cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383298} {:answer/id #uuid "261e6f16-3b2e-587e-8d2d-fe99babfa8e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba51663a-cf49-5ab7-996a-75a703853cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383298} :answer-field/id #uuid "76937d7a-eb36-5682-9b24-95f020347fbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "465664e9-9e79-5042-b31f-cc26731fd988" :question/math-academy-id "q-339692" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,(-\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,(\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee42bc5-fa4b-551c-bfeb-27613fe50f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317511} {:answer/id #uuid "2950a4c5-4cd2-5fdf-83a1-9e5ed2a6947c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ee42bc5-fa4b-551c-bfeb-27613fe50f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317511} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cdbd09-f4a6-5047-95aa-51ace608e3b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3808e545-f492-5548-9d11-622244ade519" :question/math-academy-id "q-339797" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e6d24c-8440-5a91-8411-d737966f2f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48e6d24c-8440-5a91-8411-d737966f2f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383302} :answer-field/id #uuid "16eb4281-0d49-524b-95c0-084d48c5bcf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6d95c69-caf3-511b-b6ef-078487caf544" :question/math-academy-id "q-339798" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb0d501-c98c-5043-b3d3-c9a419627127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cb0d501-c98c-5043-b3d3-c9a419627127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383305} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf945d2-2c69-587d-9983-3978602a98f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c70f0c51-11a4-5b22-b4c6-7f265fa6778d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340137" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d3812b-a432-5f17-b78b-08135e18dd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d3812b-a432-5f17-b78b-08135e18dd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317519} :answer-field/id #uuid "84fbab3d-df13-55e6-9bee-693cbdd829aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "186dc72e-1eb6-57fe-a676-4e381bb03180" :question/math-academy-id "q-340150" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4} & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bae46d23-d881-5561-be1a-7cfd57071b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076318} {:answer/id #uuid "05443e50-d9b9-5afb-9a98-ac3524e8d7e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076319} {:answer/id #uuid "2bcf3d7a-b343-58b1-9558-0f23b3cfb04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076320} {:answer/id #uuid "63819c76-f0b7-59fa-a59f-32ae2e246389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076321} {:answer/id #uuid "6df6a904-db7b-5270-8157-c28717ac47e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6df6a904-db7b-5270-8157-c28717ac47e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076322} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da7469b-156d-54ac-b961-bdc8d97c21b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b373dda-fb35-59ea-9255-3dda08116944" :question/math-academy-id "q-4487" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d732378-ed53-54b3-8292-10e547a1aa76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383308} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f3d4fc-d6c0-5b44-a57d-d894cc19118e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383309} {:answer/id #uuid "b5561092-3103-521e-9773-1a9ad1c9dc93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383310} {:answer/id #uuid "46c6cbbf-a3df-5b91-bffa-92ae0ccd890d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383311} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd7bf15-174e-5e85-aa81-be24d9dc6a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9f3d4fc-d6c0-5b44-a57d-d894cc19118e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383309} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d452c0-f884-575b-b2ab-2e5de92e14ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8447edc0-a30b-5171-961e-2a8384886c4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73548" :question/problem "Find the value of ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ÷\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{-1} \\\\  & ={1}^{-1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
